// PreToolUse guard on the `Agent` tool: write the dispatch row, and stamp the
// dispatch key into the prompt so the close side can find it (mt#2292).
//
// ## Why this guard exists at all
//
// `subagent_invocations` had accumulated writers on every path EXCEPT the one
// that actually carries the traffic. `tasks_dispatch` and
// `session_generate_prompt` both write rows, but neither SPAWNS anything — the
// spawn is the raw harness `Agent` tool, which touches no Minsky code. Measured
// 2026-08-05: 208 rows, 47 closed (22.6%). This guard sits on the raw path.
//
// ## The two halves, and why both are needed
//
//  1. **Write the dispatch row.** The PreToolUse payload carries `tool_use_id`,
//     which is the SAME key `agent_spawns.parent_tool_use_id` already uses — so
//     the row keys on harness-provided identity that the transcript pipeline
//     also keys on, rather than on a token this task invents. A bespoke id would
//     have left the two tables unjoinable.
//  2. **Stamp that key into the prompt** via `updatedInput`. This is the part
//     that looks redundant and is not. The Stop side gets `agent_id` and
//     `agent_transcript_path` but NOT `tool_use_id`; the dispatch side gets
//     `tool_use_id` but not `agent_id` (the subagent does not exist yet).
//     Nothing in either payload joins them. Writing the key into the prompt puts
//     it in the child's own transcript, which is the one artifact both sides can
//     address.
//
// ## Ordering: the stamp does not depend on the write
//
// The stamp is computed from the payload alone and returned even when the DB
// write fails or times out. That is deliberate — a lost stamp is unrecoverable
// (the prompt is already sent), whereas a lost dispatch row degrades to a
// Stop-side INSERT that still carries the outcome. The cheap, irreversible half
// must not be gated on the expensive, recoverable one.
//
// ## Posture
//
// NEVER denies, and fails open on every error. A dispatch must not be blocked by
// an observability writer; that would trade the failure this task fixes for a
// strictly worse one.
//
// Diagnostics go to `auditLines` (stderr), never stdout: Claude Code discards a
// PreToolUse hook's ENTIRE output when stdout carries anything besides the one
// JSON object (mt#3625), and this guard's output includes `updatedInput` — so a
// stray stdout write here would silently drop the stamp it exists to emit.

